---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import RelatedTools from '../components/RelatedTools.astro';
import SocialTool from '../components/SocialTool.tsx';
import Hero from '../components/Hero.astro';
import { webApplicationLd, proseClass } from './_seo';
const title = 'YouTube Thumbnail Resizer, No Upload | SizeTune';
const description =
  "Fit a picture to YouTube's thumbnail sizes for videos and Shorts, and keep the file under the 2 MB mobile limit, in your browser. Sizes come from YouTube Help.";
const jsonLd = webApplicationLd('SizeTune: YouTube thumbnail resizer', '/youtube-thumbnail-resizer/', description);
---
<Base pageType="tool" width="wide" title={title} description={description} path="/youtube-thumbnail-resizer/" jsonLd={jsonLd}>
  <Hero title="YouTube thumbnail resizer" lead="Fit a picture to a YouTube thumbnail size and keep the file within YouTube's size limit. The sizes come from YouTube Help, and nothing is uploaded." />

  <SocialTool client:load platform="youtube" />
  <noscript>
    <p class="mt-4 rounded-xl border border-amber-400 bg-amber-50 p-4">
      This tool fits images inside your browser, so it will not run while JavaScript is turned off. Enable JavaScript and
      reload to use it. The notes below on YouTube's thumbnail rules still apply with any other editor.
    </p>
  </noscript>
  <p class="mx-auto mt-6 max-w-3xl text-center text-base text-slate-700">
    Want a different file size? <a data-testid="to-resizer" class="font-medium text-teal-800 underline underline-offset-4" href="/">Open the KB resizer</a>.
  </p>

  <div class={`mx-auto max-w-3xl mt-10 ${proseClass}`}>
    <h2>What YouTube asks for</h2>
    <p>
      YouTube Help recommends a video thumbnail of 3840 by 2160 pixels in the 16:9 shape, and says it should be as large
      as possible, with a minimum width of 640 pixels. For Shorts the recommended size is 2160 by 3840 in the tall 9:16
      shape, with a minimum height of 640. The help page names JPG and PNG as accepted image formats. This tool saves a
      JPEG.
    </p>

    <h2>Channel banner</h2>
    <p>
      The size list also has a channel banner. YouTube Help recommends 2560 by 1440 pixels in the 16:9 shape, with 2048 by
      1152 as the smallest size it accepts, and asks for a file of 6 MB or smaller. Text and logos should stay inside a
      safe area of 1235 by 338 pixels in the middle, because the banner is cropped on some screens, such as a phone.
      Choosing the banner size sets the size limit to 6,000 KB for you. A picture smaller than 2560 by 1440 is enlarged,
      and the tool tells you so, because an enlarged banner looks soft.
    </p>

    <h2>The file size limit</h2>
    <p>
      The same page gives two limits. A video thumbnail can be up to 2 MB when you set it from the mobile app, and up to
      50 MB from a computer. Because you may not know which you will use, the tool starts with a size limit of 2,000 KB,
      which fits both. If your result is larger than that, the quality is lowered to try to fit it, and the page says whether it did so. Clear
      the box for no limit, or enter another number.
    </p>
    <p>
      A 3840 by 2160 picture is big, and a detailed one can need lower quality to fit under 2 MB. If it looks too soft,
      a smaller picture at higher quality can look better, but check that it stays above the 640 pixel minimum.
    </p>

    <h2>Fitting a picture that is not 16:9</h2>
    <p>
      Photos from a phone or camera are often 4:3 or square, and a screenshot may be any shape. Crop fills the whole
      thumbnail and cuts off the rest, and you choose whether the top, middle or bottom of the picture stays, which is
      useful when a face is near one edge. Colour bars keep the whole picture with bars at the sides. A blurred
      background keeps the whole picture and fills the sides with a soft copy of it, a look many channels use for
      pictures that are not widescreen.
    </p>

    <h2>Thumbnails are read small</h2>
    <p>
      Most viewers see a thumbnail as a small card in a list. Keep the main subject large and near the middle, use few
      words of large text, and keep the text short. The tool shows a preview so you can step back from the screen and see whether it still reads.
    </p>

    <h2>Frequently asked questions about YouTube thumbnails</h2>
    <h3>Why does the tool use 2,000 KB as a default?</h3>
    <p>YouTube Help gives 2 MB as the mobile limit and 50 MB for a computer. The smaller number is safe for both.</p>
    <h3>Do I have to use 3840 by 2160?</h3>
    <p>It is the recommended size. YouTube Help says the minimum width is 640 pixels, and a larger picture gives a sharper result.</p>
    <h3>Can I make a Shorts thumbnail?</h3>
    <p>Yes. Pick the Shorts size, which is the tall 2160 by 3840 shape.</p>
    <h3>Why is my result a JPEG?</h3>
    <p>JPEG is small and accepted by YouTube. This tool saves only JPEG, so a PNG with transparency is flattened onto the colour you choose.</p>
    <h3>Is my picture uploaded?</h3>
    <p>
      No. It is fitted in your browser. See the <a href="/privacy/">privacy page</a>. The{' '}
      <a href="/instagram-image-resizer/">Instagram</a> and <a href="/linkedin-image-resizer/">LinkedIn</a> pages cover
      those platforms.
    </p>
  </div>
  <RelatedTools path="/youtube-thumbnail-resizer/" />
</Base>
